#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133899 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133715 :question/id #uuid "18eef985-0fd4-5b08-8072-684ded227ab4" :question/math-academy-id "e-9557" :question/problem "![](images/16/16026d4ac7deeb30c1abb3a066df23a06a544116463b364b0a1e9e0bac0608c8.png)\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r = 10, \n\\qquad \n\\oint\\limits_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r = -8, \n\\qquad \n\\oint\\limits_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r = 3,\n$$\n\nwhere $C_1$ and $C_3$ are oriented clockwise, and $C_2$ is oriented counterclockwise, calculate the value of $$\n\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{\\partial D}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve $C_1$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse $C_1$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves $C_2$ and $C_3$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse $C_2$ and $C_3$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/11/11445c083440ee2fde1864d599a8bbd68146d522c61de11b3e90dc231e6eddf1.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientations of the outer curve $C_1$ and the inner curve $C_2$ are opposite those stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-10-(-8)+3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "56caab23-f54d-58ee-99db-99c651f78e5c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050773 :topic/difficulty 0.6850848950417171 :topic/id #uuid "659072cc-8b2c-5e8d-b6a6-88321581f47f" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179740} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179743}] :topic/math-academy-id 3713 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051070}] :topic/title "Circulation"}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186731019 :question-generator/id #uuid "5314ed07-cf50-51f5-b627-e5fd65d0cdd4" :question-generator/script "generators/34/346dcfcfb54e4ac2b1f35d8f710b0d88c871653f5ec3fa647f2c6a805c8662d4/17592186133899.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134098 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc41a40b-917a-57ad-93c5-f9db0205c2ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130746} {:answer/id #uuid "34e14694-9434-5ad9-aa2d-2969b9ae1cc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130747} {:answer/id #uuid "4b2ce2f4-0374-5148-947b-96f3d8b6b4ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130748} {:answer/id #uuid "5d7d99a7-294b-5cf9-90af-ed4075071bb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130749} {:answer/id #uuid "323a1bfd-c415-5a52-8f82-c95d150e0d2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441592}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34e14694-9434-5ad9-aa2d-2969b9ae1cc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130747} :answer-field/id #uuid "59065be7-12d4-5585-856f-70e63556d4de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130744}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aa43bdf8-83a8-50c7-a713-6a675dec7cf1" :question/math-academy-id "q-126558" :question/problem "![](images/72/7264984a1c19c247c030feae04615bb0c46205ca70475db1240db964ad6007b2.png)\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=10,\\hspace{2em}{∮}_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=-4,\\hspace{2em}{∮}_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}=-2,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of $$\n{∮}_{∂D}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/0d/0de833fcc39b6295418c1558cf1187774db7b4ada1d697f2dcac8c35858563df.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =10-4-2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134102 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8dac37b-0c3a-5985-b30b-b455cf17748a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130769} {:answer/id #uuid "0f82c5b5-9ec9-5493-9c5e-088d8f3b2063" :answer/type {:db/ide